This study investigates solutions to the elliptic PDE system in R^2, given by \begin{align*}
\begin{cases}
\Delta u + u = Q(x) + \mu u^3 + o(\mu), \\
\Delta v + v = u + \mu v^3 + o(\mu),
\end{align*}where (u, v) represents a pair of Allen-Cahn phase separation patterns, modified by \mu. The main achievement of this study is the discovery of a minimal, radially symmetric solution (u_0, v_0) for the original PDE system, given specific symmetry conditions for the potential function Q. Furthermore, this finding is complemented by an analysis of the local uniqueness of (u_0, v_0), along with estimates for the size of u_0, demonstrating its finite magnitude. The most critical aspect of this research is the identification of a minimal solution (u_0, v_0) for the original PDE system, provided that the potential function Q exhibits certain symmetry properties.
